For the simplified axially symmetric rotor, $H_{\mathrm{rot}}
  =\frac{1}{2I}
  \left(L_x^2+L_y^2+L_z^2\right)
  =\frac{L^2}{2I}.$ The coefficient is $1/(2I)$, not $I/2$; that slip is made and corrected at the board. Using Eq. [eq:l8-angular-algebra], $\begin{aligned}\dot L_x
  &=\{L_x,H_{\mathrm{rot}}\}\\
  &=\frac{1}{2I}\left(
    2L_y\{L_x,L_y\}
    +2L_z\{L_x,L_z\}
  \right)\\
  &=\frac{1}{I}\left(L_yL_z-L_zL_y\right)=0.
  \end{aligned}$ Cyclically, $\dot L_x=\dot L_y=\dot L_z=0.$ The scalar $L^2$ has zero bracket with each component $L_i$; in the language of this algebra, it is a Casimir. Thus a torque-free gyroscope keeps both the magnitude and direction of its angular momentum.

